Subject: add vendor and /run tiers to the default configuration search
Source: downstream patch for popt 1.19
Upstream: not submitted; needs agreement on the /usr/share/popt and
  /run/popt paths

poptReadDefaultConfig() (src/poptconfig.c) compiled a single system
tier, POPT_SYSCONFDIR "/popt" plus its POPT_SYSCONFDIR "/popt.d/*"
drop-ins. src/Makefile.am defines POPT_SYSCONFDIR from
-DPOPT_SYSCONFDIR="\"$(sysconfdir)\"", and the package manifest ran
./configure --prefix=/usr with no --sysconfdir, so autotools kept its
${prefix}/etc default and POPT_SYSCONFDIR compiled to /usr/etc: a path
under the immutable /usr tree that a machine owner can never write.
There was also no vendor or /run tier, so a package could not ship a
default alias and a transient override had nowhere to go.

This patch factors the pre-existing single-tier body of
poptReadDefaultConfig() into a new static poptReadConfigTier(), then
calls it three times for /usr/share/popt (vendor), /run/popt (boot),
and POPT_SYSCONFDIR (administrator, /etc once the manifest passes
--sysconfdir=/etc). Each tier keeps the exact poptSaneFile() filtering
and HAVE_GLOB_H glob-guard structure the single /etc tier already had,
and poptReadConfigFile() already treats a missing base file as success
(poptReadFile() returns ENOENT, which poptReadConfigFile() maps to rc
== 0), while poptReadConfigTier() only globs a drop-in directory that
stat() confirms exists, so a missing vendor, /run, or /etc tier stays
silent exactly as the missing /etc tier already was.

poptAddItem() (src/popt.c) always appends a new alias or exec entry to
the end of con->aliases / con->execs, and handleAlias() (src/popt.c)
looks up a name by walking that array backward from its end ("for (i =
con->numAliases - 1; i >= 0; i--)"), so the entry added last is the
entry a lookup finds first. Reading vendor first, then /run, then the
administrator's POPT_SYSCONFDIR tier last therefore makes an
administrator alias beat the same alias read from /run, which beats
the vendor default. The per-user $HOME/.popt file keeps its current
meaning: poptReadDefaultConfig() still reads it last, after all three
system tiers, so it keeps overriding every one of them.

Neither new tier installs anything under /etc or /usr/etc:
/usr/share/popt and /run/popt are only compiled-in search paths that
this package does not populate.
